1
00:00:00,760 --> 00:00:04,160
Hello, I’m Luyao Zhang from Duke Kunshan University.

2
00:00:04,160 --> 00:00:08,017
My digital avatar will walk you through a stablecoin alert.

3
00:00:08,347 --> 00:00:14,560
Imagine running a DAO treasury. One USDC now buys only 0.9930 USDT.

4
00:00:14,560 --> 00:00:18,269
Should a colleague review it right away?

5
00:00:18,599 --> 00:00:22,462
I wanted to answer two questions. Did the alert actually happen?

6
00:00:22,462 --> 00:00:25,359
What evidence makes it worth a colleague’s time?

7
00:00:26,119 --> 00:00:33,212
The center line is parity. The green band extends half a percent on either side.

8
00:00:33,542 --> 00:00:38,233
The control quote, 0.9990, is ten basis points below parity.

9
00:00:38,233 --> 00:00:43,628
The animation’s 0.9950 sits exactly on the boundary. Both stay quiet.

10
00:00:43,958 --> 00:00:47,428
At 0.9930, the gap reaches seventy basis points.

11
00:00:47,428 --> 00:00:51,839
Crossing the threshold makes the contract request a callback.

12
00:00:52,599 --> 00:00:58,514
These are actual testnet transactions. I published the simulated quote on Ethereum Sepolia.

13
00:00:58,514 --> 00:01:02,220
The Lasna contract subscribes to this publisher’s events.

14
00:01:02,550 --> 00:01:06,587
Lasna checks the source and threshold, then requests a callback.

15
00:01:06,587 --> 00:01:11,507
The recipient on Base Sepolia records the alert. Both steps run automatically.

16
00:01:11,837 --> 00:01:17,055
To match the event, compare the source transaction hash and log index two.

17
00:01:17,055 --> 00:01:21,427
The three UTC block times are 13:38:12, 13:38:12 and 13:38:14.

18
00:01:21,757 --> 00:01:26,098
The first and last block timestamps differ by two seconds in this run.

19
00:01:26,098 --> 00:01:28,517
Each receipt has its own explorer link.

20
00:01:29,277 --> 00:01:31,940
An off-chain model prioritizes review.

21
00:01:31,940 --> 00:01:38,597
It needs earlier pair history and contemporaneous independent dollar references for both coins.

22
00:01:38,927 --> 00:01:45,271
The public run had no dollar references. The alert was retained and the score withheld.

23
00:01:45,271 --> 00:01:47,386
That is the observed outcome.

24
00:01:47,716 --> 00:01:54,085
For the two alternatives I keep the on-chain alert fixed and switch labeled synthetic evidence.

25
00:01:54,085 --> 00:01:59,985
Corroborating dollar weakness escalates review. Near-par references support observation.

26
00:02:00,745 --> 00:02:04,855
The tool records alerts and moves zero stablecoin principal.

27
00:02:04,855 --> 00:02:10,129
Repeating a source log sends one callback. Redelivering it records one alert.

28
00:02:10,459 --> 00:02:14,448
The newer contracts cap both individual and lifetime service payments.

29
00:02:14,448 --> 00:02:19,064
An empty balance or budget stops new work. Top-ups do not reset the lifetime cap.

30
00:02:19,394 --> 00:02:24,865
All fifteen local tests passed, including valid runs, rejection, replay and recovery.

31
00:02:24,865 --> 00:02:28,791
The public receipts shown earlier predate the added fee caps.

32
00:02:29,551 --> 00:02:31,511
Back to the treasury desk.

33
00:02:31,511 --> 00:02:38,901
At 0.9930 the team can first verify the three receipts, then identify the missing market evidence.

34
00:02:39,231 --> 00:02:44,884
Next I want to connect independent market feeds and measure how many alerts warrant a human review.

35
00:02:44,884 --> 00:02:49,280
The repository contains the code, receipts and reproduction steps. Thank you.

36
00:02:50,760 --> 00:02:52,832
This is a prerecorded simulated Q&A.

37
00:02:52,832 --> 00:02:56,804
First question: how can a judge verify that the destination executed?

38
00:02:57,134 --> 00:03:03,077
Open the Base Sepolia receipt and find the recipient’s PriceDeviation event.

39
00:03:03,077 --> 00:03:09,099
Match its source transaction hash and log index two to the event ID on Lasna.

40
00:03:09,429 --> 00:03:11,639
This verifies that the alert was recorded.

41
00:03:11,639 --> 00:03:15,848
Two seconds is the difference between the endpoint block timestamps in this run.

42
00:03:16,608 --> 00:03:19,797
Second question: what work does Reactive actually do?

43
00:03:20,127 --> 00:03:27,984
It subscribes to the designated contract, checks source, threshold and event identity, then requests the cross-chain callback.

44
00:03:27,984 --> 00:03:32,786
Otherwise the team would operate its own listener, relay and replay controls.

45
00:03:33,116 --> 00:03:35,676
I signed two quotes during the public test.

46
00:03:35,676 --> 00:03:41,334
Once the threshold was crossed, the relay and target write needed no further signature from me.

47
00:03:42,094 --> 00:03:47,438
Third question: does one pool quote establish a depeg?

48
00:03:47,768 --> 00:03:50,810
One pool quote warrants investigation.

49
00:03:50,810 --> 00:03:56,734
The model also needs history and independent dollar prices for both coins.

50
00:03:56,734 --> 00:04:01,857
The public run kept the alert and identified the missing inputs.

51
00:04:02,187 --> 00:04:10,175
Holding the same alert fixed, we can change the two dollar references and test whether the recommendation changes.

52
00:04:10,935 --> 00:04:16,288
Final question: what if a callback fails or an event arrives twice?

53
00:04:16,618 --> 00:04:20,949
A failed call leaves the destination count unchanged.

54
00:04:20,949 --> 00:04:26,505
Inspect the proxy, target event, balance, debt and remaining budget.

55
00:04:26,505 --> 00:04:30,508
Authorized redelivery can record that event once.

56
00:04:30,838 --> 00:04:34,723
You can rerun all fifteen tests from the repository.

57
00:04:34,723 --> 00:04:39,280
Please collect additional questions for my written follow-up.
